\par
Theme: British attitudes towards empire\par
Popular attitudes towards the empire, 1857\endash 1968\par
\par
Date: 1857\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Arguments of John Mackenzie, *Propaganda and Empire*: Mackenzie sees the Indian Mutiny as an \lquote ideological turning point\rquote  from when the British public became more interested in empire.\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* Throughout this period from the early 19th century there was sustained opposition to the slave trade \endash  Victorians were proud to pose as liberators of slaves.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Limited appearance of empire or imperial themes in the arts and high culture (no \lquote canonical\rquote  novel is set in the empire) in the 19th century.\par
\par
Date: 1860\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* David Livingstone\rquote s writings and exploration fascinated the British public \endash  he became a great hero. Idealisation of missionary culture.\par
* Developments in media and literacy, plus the widening of the franchise (1867) enabled greater communication and information about the empire.\par
* Close interest in 3rd Ashanti War (covered by Henty \endash  first \lquote media war\rquote ).\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* 1865 \endash  Some public opposition to the actions of Governor Eyre in the aftermath of the Jamaica rebellion.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Limited appearance of imperial themes in popular culture (before GA Henty).\par
* Schools (including public schools) rarely taught children anything about empire due to focus on Classics and liberty in British history.\par
\par
Date: 1870\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Disraeli wins 1874 election having placed imperialism at the centre of Conservative party policy.\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* 1880 \endash  Gladstone\rquote s Midlothian campaign criticised Disraeli\rquote s expansionist imperial policy \endash  Gladstone won the election.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Porter argues class was more important than empire; governing of empire was carried out by a tiny elite.\par
\par
Date: 1880\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Pressure placed on Gladstone to occupy Egypt in 1882.\par
* 1885 \endash  Pressure placed on Gladstone to send relief mission to save General Gordon (fails: Gladstone unpopular).\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* During Boer War, Emily Hobhouse leads criticism of British use of concentration camps. Lloyd George among others labelled \lquote pro-Boers\rquote  for criticising government policy.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Porter argues ordinary people were preoccupied with daily concerns and not particularly interested in empire.\par
\par
Date: 1890\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Growing popularity of authors like Rudyard Kipling and GA Henty.\par
* Advertising (e.g., Bovril) increasingly uses imperial themes.\par
* Daily Mail founded 1896 \endash  focused on imperial issues.\par
* 1897 \endash  Popularity of Victoria\rquote s Diamond Jubilee \endash  an expressly imperial occasion.\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* 1902 \endash  J. Hobson publishes *Imperialism* \endash  critiques imperialism as capitalist greed and a distraction from socialism.\par
* 1906 election decisively rejects Chamberlain\rquote s \lquote Imperial Preference\rquote  and High Tory imperialism.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* One London MP in 1902 asked schoolchildren about the Indian Mutiny \endash  only one had heard of it.\par
* Little imperial architecture in London; failed 1904 plan to build Imperial Monumental Halls and Tower.\par
* Boer War sparked more deliberate propaganda \endash  jingoism not seen as a reliable sign of enthusiasm.\par
\par
Date: 1900\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Relief of Mafeking triggers outburst of jingoism (Mafeking Night).\par
* 1900 Khaki election capitalised on military success.\par
* Empire Day started in 1902.\par
* Boy Scouts founded 1908.\par
* Popularity of Elgar\rquote s *Land of Hope and Glory* (1901).\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* See above for Boer War criticisms and Hobson\rquote s work.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Empire-themed activities like Scouts and Empire Day had other attractions beyond imperialism.\par
\par
Date: 1910\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* 1911 \endash  George V\rquote s coronation and Festival of Empire.\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* Amritsar massacre prompted significant moral debate and criticism of Dyer\rquote s actions.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Limited electoral appeal of imperial issues post-1918; often not prominent in elections.\par
\par
Date: 1920\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* British Empire Exhibition (Wembley, 1924\endash 25): 26 million visitors.\par
* Continuing popularity of Kipling, Henty; films like *Lives of a Bengal Lancer* (1935); Elgar\rquote s *Pageant of Empire* (1924).\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* Empire Marketing Board (1926) had limited success.\par
* Shift to the term \lquote Commonwealth\rquote  suggests \lquote empire\rquote  was less publicly acceptable; emphasis on trusteeship.\par
* Mockery of High Imperialism: Colonel Blimp (1934 onwards), Strachey\rquote s *Eminent Victorians* (1918).\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* See above about electoral irrelevance and limited mass engagement.\par
\par
Date: 1930\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Silver Jubilee of George V and coronation of George VI.\par
* British Empire Exhibition (Glasgow, 1938): 12 million visitors.\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* Continued mockery of old imperialism and attempts to reframe it as modernity.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Limited visibility in high or popular culture except through rebranded formats.\par
\par
Date: 1940\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Continued popularity of Empire Day.\par
* Coronation of Elizabeth II (1953) featured troops and dignitaries from across empire.\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* After 1945, popular rejection of racism and imperialism \endash  associated with Axis powers.\par
* Rise of satire and establishment criticism, especially post-Suez.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* 1948 survey: half the British population couldn\rquote t name a single colony.\par
* Broad post-war consensus on decolonisation; little electoral concern over losing colonies.\par
\par
Date: 1950\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Some continuation of imperial themes in films: *North-West Frontier* (1959), *Lawrence of Arabia* (1962), *Zulu* (1964).\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* Suez Crisis leads to Conservative defeat in 1963.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Popular culture in the 1950s\endash 60s leaned more towards American media and internal British developments than imperial themes.\par
\par
Date: 1960\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* 1965 \endash  Funeral of Churchill seen as a final imperial moment.\par
\par
Evidence of hostility towards imperialism:\par
\par
* Post-war consensus on decolonisation continues.\par
\par
Evidence of apathy/ignorance of empire:\par
\par
* Lack of concern for imperial decline; contrasted with reactions in France and Portugal.\par
\par
Date: 1970\par
Evidence of support for imperialism:\par
\par
* Some view support for Falklands War (1982) as a legacy of imperial jingoism.\par
\par
